{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And if of the fowl.</b> This too adds to the first matter, to apply everything stated above to it, except for the specifics that the verse explicitly excluded from it. The first is that a bird comes only as an olah, from what is deduced from ״the fowl, an olah,״ which changed from the order of the olah of cattle and sheep — or according to the reading of the extra word — and further from the implication of ״his offering,״ that a bird olah does not come from the congregation. Also the rule of melikah, which is a disqualification dependent on the kohen, whereas slaughter is not a disqualification in the olah of cattle and sheep. Also the specifics that were repeated in the olah of cattle and the olah of sheep without need — these come to exclude the bird. But other things that the verse excluded in one of the two and derived one from the other, such as nirba, muktzah, ne'evad, terefah, ill, old, stolen — all are disqualified in a bird just as in an animal, as is clarified in the words of the Sages and in the Rambam in chapter 3 of Hilkhot Issurei Mizbeach. Therefore it says ״And if of the fowl.״" "<b>His offering to YHWH.</b> The reason it says <b>his offering to YHWH</b>, which it did not say regarding the olah of cattle and sheep, is because we find that the verse disqualified birds with blemishes — as they said, of blessed memory (Torat Kohanim, Kiddushin 24a): there is no requirement of maleness or wholeness in a bird — as we have written above. If so, reason would suggest that this offering is inferior, for it has a blemish, and it would not fall under the category of ״an offering to YHWH.״ Therefore it says <b>his offering to YHWH</b>, for there is no lesser gradation whatsoever from this perspective, and a blemish leaves no impression at all." "It further intends, along the lines of what is said (Isaiah 57:15) ״and the crushed and lowly of spirit,״ and they said, of blessed memory (Sotah 5a): ״I am with the crushed״ — and according to the one who says ״with me is the crushed.״ For this very reason His name, may He be blessed, is not mentioned in proximity to the mention of an offering except in the case of a bird offering, for one whose way it is to bring a bird is the poor person, who brings it with a broken heart — both on account of his being poor and on account of the smallness of the offering — and He declares over him ״and the crushed,״ which is not the case with one who offers cattle and sheep, whose soul rejoices in offering something worthy of honor. And similar to this they expounded in Zevachim (Menachot 104b) regarding what the verse says concerning a meal offering, ״a soul״ — see there."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:52:35.838197209Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}